//! Swiss Ephemeris ayanamsa oracle — live cross-validation harness.
//!
//! Every reference value in `SWISS_ORACLE` below was generated by running the
//! Swiss Ephemeris itself (via pyswisseph 2.10.03, SE data files present), NOT
//! invented. The exact, reproducible command is:
//!
//! ```python
//! import swisseph as swe
//! years = [1900, 1950, 2000, 2050, 2100]
//! jds = {y: swe.julday(y, 1, 1, 12.0) for y in years}   # noon, treated as JD(TT)
//! for sid in range(0, 47):                                # SE_SIDM_* 0..46
//!     swe.set_sid_mode(sid, 0, 0)
//!     for y in years:
//!         retflags, aya = swe.get_ayanamsa_ex(jds[y], 0)  # flags=0 => WITH nutation
//!         print(sid, y, aya)                              # retflags == 2 (SEFLG_SWIEPH)
//! ```
//!
//! Notes on the call:
//!   * `flags = 0` means the apparent (with-nutation) ayanamsa — the same
//!     quantity XALEN's dynamic `TrueChitra` reduction targets. NO `SEFLG_NONUT`.
//!   * The return tuple order is `(retflags, aya)` — the ayanamsa is the SECOND
//!     element. (Reversing it silently yields the retflag 2.0 for every row.)
//!   * `swe.julday(y, 1, 1, 12.0)` gives the JDs baked into `JDS` below:
//!       1900 -> 2415021.0, 1950 -> 2433283.0, 2000 -> 2451545.0,
//!       2050 -> 2469808.0, 2100 -> 2488070.0.
//!
//! What this validates: `Ayanamsa::compute_deg` reproduces Swiss
//! `get_ayanamsa_ex(jd, 0)` (the WITH-nutation apparent ayanamsa) to under 1"
//! across 1900–2100 for every SE id (one documented 2" exception, SE id 36 —
//! see `all_ayanamsas_match_swiss_within_1_arcsec`). Each system uses the same
//! reduction Swiss does:
//!   * fixed-epoch systems: ayan_t0 + [accumulated IAU 2006 general precession
//!     from t0] + IAU 2000B nutation in longitude (Δψ);
//!   * galactic + true-star systems (incl. TrueChitra/TrueRevati/TruePushya/
//!     TrueMula and the galactic-centre / galactic-equator frames): a fixed
//!     J2000 reference direction rotated to the ecliptic of date (IAU 2006/P03)
//!     plus Δψ.
//! This replaced an earlier single-rate-linear / hand-typed-constant
//! approximation that diverged from ~17" (nutation-omitted) to >100000"
//! (GalacticCenterCochrane's J2000 anchor was 28.5° wrong — Swiss places that
//! ayanamsa near 356.85°, not 25.36°).

